diff --git a/docs/webhooks.md b/docs/webhooks.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..954467b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/webhooks.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Board webhooks
+
+Board admins can configure webhooks in the existing board settings. Each active
+webhook receives a JSON POST when an event on that board matches its subscribed
+actions. The payload contains the event ID, action, timestamp, board, creator,
+and card or comment. Links include the account's URL prefix.
+
+Requests include:
+
+- `X-Webhook-Id`: the stable delivery ID, suitable as a receiver's deduplication key.
+- `X-Webhook-Timestamp`: the attempt timestamp in UTC.
+- `X-Webhook-Signature`: the hexadecimal HMAC-SHA256 of the exact request body,
+  using the webhook's signing secret.
+
+Delivery records are created in the event transaction; rolled-back events send
+nothing. Jobs are queued after commit. A unique database index prevents duplicate
+records for a webhook/event pair, and an atomic pending-to-in-progress transition
+allows only one worker to make the POST. Neither jobs nor the HTTP client retry
+an attempted delivery. Redirects are not followed, and private, local, and other
+reserved network destinations are blocked. DNS results are pinned to the HTTP
+connection to prevent DNS rebinding.
+
+This is a **single-attempt delivery** guarantee, not a guarantee of exactly-once
+processing by an arbitrary HTTP receiver. A timeout or worker crash can leave the
+remote outcome unknown; resending in that case could process the event twice.
+Receivers that require exactly-once effects must persist the delivery ID and apply
+their effects idempotently. A worker crash after claiming leaves an `in_progress`
+log rather than risking a duplicate POST.
+
+The board settings show recent deliveries and their HTTP status or error. The
+existing deliveries JSON endpoint exposes sanitized request headers and a
+response summary; signatures are not exposed there. Stored responses include
+headers and up to 10 KiB of body. HTTP 2xx responses succeed; other statuses and
+network errors fail.
+
+A receiver is deactivated when it has at least ten consecutive failures and the
+streak has lasted at least one hour. A success clears the streak. Reactivating a
+webhook clears its old streak too. Queued deliveries to an inactive webhook are
+logged as skipped errors and do not contact it.
+
+The existing recurring cleanup task removes delivery logs older than one week
+in batches, every fifteen minutes. Failure tracking is independent of log
+retention and is removed when the webhook is deleted.
